WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.195 --> 00:00:03.380
(hip hop music)

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:05.800
- Hey guys, I wanted to bring you part two

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.070
of my medical journey.

00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.040
When I made part one talking about college

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.100
and medical school,

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.210
I asked if you wanted to
see the rest of my journey,

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.370
talking about residency, social
media, traditional media,

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:17.170
and you seemed to really want it.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.570
So here we go, let's get started.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.340
I'm gonna talk about everything
from medical school on.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.100
I think a good place to jump in here

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.730
would be to talk about the Match process.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:27.770
I know that gives people a lot of anxiety

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:29.150
when we talk about the Match,

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:33.090
because it's complicated,
it's a major life moment

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.460
that decides where you're gonna
be for the next few years,

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:36.960
and I totally understand that.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:40.830
And especially, there's
a lot of changes going on

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.670
with the current Match system,

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.180
because the DO Match and the MD Match

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.520
are actually combining,

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:49.100
and are gonna become one Match in 2020,

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:51.960
so that brings an extra layer
of anxiety along with it.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.910
When I was going through
the Match process,

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.400
you had to decide if you were
gonna forgo the DO Match,

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.670
and go straight to the MD Match,

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.230
or you can participate in
both Matches, actually.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:03.640
I've seen some people do that.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.640
They would pick a couple of
their favorite DO programs.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.870
And if they didn't get into those,

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:10.580
then they would
automatically be pushed over

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:11.620
into the MD Match.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.950
I was going only for the DO Match.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.000
I didn't wanna do the MD Match,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.950
because I really believe
in the DO philosophy.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:21.280
I wanted to train under
osteopathic physicians,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.870
and continue honing my DO skills.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:25.520
Not only do I believe in the philosophy

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.280
of treating a patient as a whole,

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.430
and everything else that comes along

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:30.700
with being an osteopath,

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.510
but also doing the
osteopathic manipulation.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.570
I've seen some great
results with my patients,

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.010
low-risk tool that I have in my tool belt

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.740
that I can use in addition
to medicines and surgery.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.830
I interviewed at probably,
it was a large amount.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.770
Most people don't interview
in as many places as I did.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.100
But I wanted to get a good
picture of what it was like

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:51.570
in each of these hospitals.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.570
So I think I interviewed at a total of

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:57.270
maybe 12 to 15 different hospitals.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:01.490
I applied to probably 30 places.

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:04.560
I went as far as interviewing in places

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.270
like Florida, just because I wanted to see

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.320
how different the healthcare system was

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:12.330
within Florida, within
New York, New Jersey,

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.010
Long Island even.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.240
And I found that my top two choices

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.900
that I really enjoyed for family medicine

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.920
were Overlook Medical Center,

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.970
and Jamaica Hospital in Queens, New York.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:27.850
What really turned the
tide for me was that

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.420
in Overlook Medical Center,

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:32.290
the residents got along really well,

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:35.360
and in residency programs,

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.120
it's not unusual to see
bickering between residents,

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:41.060
especially because it's such
a stressful time in your life.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.330
You think about how many hours
you're committing to work.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.780
These people essentially
become your family.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.520
So it's really important
that you get along

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:49.800
with your co-residents,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.030
because you're gonna be spending

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.840
a lot, a lot of time with them.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.710
I saw the relationship
between the residents,

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.250
I saw that they were being true.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:00.380
They weren't putting on a
show during interview day.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.210
And when you go on some
of your interviews,

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:03.669
if you're going through this process,

00:03:03.669 --> 00:03:05.490
you're gonna see a lot
of fake stuff happen.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.630
You're gonna see program directors,

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:10.770
you're gonna see residents,
you're gonna see secretaries,

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.290
they're gonna put on shows for you.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:13.630
And I understand that,

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:15.390
if you're trying to make a
good impression on somebody,

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.520
you're gonna put your best foot forward.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.200
But what was nice in Overlook,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.300
I saw that the residents
weren't putting on a show.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.840
They were honest, they
really were friends.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.780
You could see that even
when you walked away

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.250
from the interview process,

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.210
you were able to see that
they were really friends.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:34.250
They were talking, they were
making plans to hang out.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:35.940
Plus, Overlook does a really great job

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:37.930
at allowing you to hang
out with the residents,

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:40.120
even go for lunch with
some of the residents.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:43.130
Something that I do encourage
everybody to take advantage of

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:46.330
if they're considering choosing
a specific residency program

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:48.810
is doing something called the second look.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:51.170
A second look is
basically when you come in

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:53.070
on a non-interview day,

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:55.530
and shadow one of the
residents around for the day

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:57.970
to see what their daily duties are like.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:03:59.630
Definitely take advantage of this.

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:02.660
Number one, you get to see
what the program is like

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:04.630
when they're not putting
their best foot forward,

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:06.040
when there may be an incident,

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.700
where they might, when
something might go wrong,

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.490
and you get to see what it's like,

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:12.460
and how they handle those situations.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:17.260
Funny enough, I think that I
did my second look for Overlook

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:20.920
on Christmas, either Christmas
or maybe Christmas Eve.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:23.270
So you would think, right,

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:26.890
you're going into a residency program

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.330
where residents are
working a major holiday.

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.310
And you would think they'd
all be unhappy, right,

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:32.470
because it's a major holiday,

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.240
they're away from their families.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:35.073
None of that.

00:04:35.073 --> 00:04:38.580
I sat in on rounds, I went
into the hospital with them,

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:39.690
I got to see what they did

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.150
on a regular basis on a given day,

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:44.070
even on a day like
Christmas or Christmas Eve,

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:45.890
whatever the holiday was.

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:48.260
And guess what, they had great attitudes,

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:49.830
they all got along very well,

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:51.570
and most importantly, they were humble.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.010
In medicine, things change all the time.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:55.320
We'll make one recommendation

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:57.660
only to change it back to
the previous recommendation

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.180
a few years later, this happens.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:00.650
We've seen this with coffee.

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:01.870
In the 80s, we were saying

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:03.970
coffee gives you pancreatic cancer.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.830
In 2017, studies are saying
it makes you live longer.

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:10.170
I think a good doctor is a
good skeptic of themselves.

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:12.390
That's why I always
highlight that a good doctor

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:15.440
should never be scared to say
I don't know, and in fact,

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.640
say that the reason that
I know this information

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:20.660
is 'cause it's based
on the best information

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:24.270
available to me now, but it
may change in the future.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.610
We have to come with
that kind of open mind

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:28.470
when we're treating our patients.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:31.620
And that's the exact type of personality

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:34.610
and leadership that I saw
in Overlook Medical Center.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.410
I saw Dr. Tribuna, who's
my program director.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:40.380
I saw Dr. Davine talk about those things.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.780
I saw that leadership happen
amongst the residents,

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:45.020
and I said, this is a
place where I wanna train.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:46.840
These are the types of
residents and leadership

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.400
I want to surround myself with.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:51.310
They were amazing with
their bedside manner,

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:54.010
they were humble, they knew
what their limitations were.

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:57.330
These are definitely characteristics

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.810
that I wanted to carry
on as a physician myself.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:04.810
After leaving my second look,
and doing the ranking process,

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:08.110
I ranked Overlook Medical
Center as my first choice,

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:12.230
Jamaica Hospital as my second choice.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:15.100
Oh, let me get into the interview portion

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.660
out of some of these hospitals,
'cause this gets funny,

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:19.050
and I don't want this to throw you.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.230
In the previous video, I talked about

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:22.920
what it means to have a good interview.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:24.510
It's all about being yourself,

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:26.510
it's all about telling your story,

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:29.930
and it's all about vocalizing
your story, and your answers

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:32.040
to yourself before you
go in for that interview.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:33.270
Some of these interviews,

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:35.030
and I'm not gonna name the programs

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:36.760
'cause I don't wanna bad-mouth anybody,

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:41.760
but they get just incredibly strange.

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:43.380
Strange is the right word for it,

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.360
because they ask really weird questions.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:50.930
I remember a doctor at a New
Jersey hospital asking me what,

00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:53.450
if I was a vegetable,
what vegetable would I be?

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:56.850
Or if I was a candy,
what candy would I be?

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:00.420
And then I remember in one
hospital in Long Island,

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.590
one of the directors, actually,
he wasn't even a director,

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:06.640
he was the behavioral
psychologist for that program,

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:08.840
asking me this series of questions,

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:09.950
and it was really strange.

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:12.880
He told me, pick a person
that you think knows you best,

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:14.790
and think about that person.

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:18.350
And then he said, now
answer these questions

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:19.410
as if you're that person.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:21.410
Let's say I said my friend Mark

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:24.880
was the person that knew me best.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:29.000
What would Mark say is
Mike's biggest strength,

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.467
a.k.a. my strength, and
then I had to answer.

00:07:31.467 --> 00:07:33.650
And then he said, what would Mark say,

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:35.160
you know, Mike's a great guy,

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:37.130
but what's his biggest weakness?

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:40.080
And he framed the whole interview

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.320
as if he was interviewing
Mark, not myself.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:46.270
This may be a psychological,

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:49.350
interesting way of interviewing somebody,

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:52.550
but it's not human,
and I didn't like that.

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:55.990
I don't like when you try and
play psychological mind games

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:56.850
during an interview.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:58.420
That's not what it's supposed to be about.

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:00.850
It's supposed to be about a
one-on-one human connection.

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:02.773
I never like those types of questions.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:06.560
When someone asks you
them, answer them openly,

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:08.510
answer them honestly, be human.

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:10.830
Don't be afraid to talk about your faults.

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:12.940
I did all those interviews.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:16.020
I picked Overlook as my number one choice.

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:19.450
I was very nervous, wondering
where I would end up,

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:22.620
because you are gonna spend the next

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.270
three years of your life at that place,

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:26.920
and I know some people
that go into very, very

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:28.253
competitive specialties.

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:30.120
Some of them are competitive

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:34.100
because there's only single
number of spots in a state,

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:36.830
so they apply all across the country,

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:39.110
and they don't know if next,

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:40.485
for the next three to five years,

00:08:40.485 --> 00:08:42.190
they're gonna be in
California, in New York,

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:43.320
in Florida, in Texas.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:46.010
You will see tears on Match Day.

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:48.060
You will see smiles on Match Day.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:53.060
It's a very, very tedious, unique process

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:55.610
that applies to the medical journey.

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:57.430
Now on my Match Day,

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:02.430
I was doing rotations in
Trinitas Hospital in New Jersey

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:06.430
for my internal medicine sub-I,

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:09.420
and a sub-I is basically a sub-internship.

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:11.060
You're basically the intern,

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:12.610
which is your first year of residency.

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:15.330
You're basically the intern's assistant,

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:17.510
and you get to do a lot of the things

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:19.780
that the average third year
medical student can't do.

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:22.610
And I was doing that, and
I got an email on my phone

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:24.280
saying that I successfully matched

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:26.180
with Overlook Medical Center.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:27.230
I was so excited.

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:29.890
I mean, to get your number one choice

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:32.760
in whatever specialty
you're going for is amazing.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:35.920
I remember Dr. Tribunal leaving
me a very kind voicemail

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:37.660
welcoming me to the program,

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:40.200
telling me how they're
very excited to have me.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.200
And something that I
really liked that he said,

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:44.600
and I still can't really
get comfortable doing,

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:48.720
is he said, from now on, it's
not Dr. Tribuna, it's Joe.

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:51.950
And he wanted to call
him by his first name.

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:53.600
And I couldn't get used to that.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:55.010
I was there for three years.

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:56.150
Every day that I would see him,

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:57.890
I would say Dr. Tribuna out of respect.

00:09:57.890 --> 00:10:00.430
And he would say, Mike, Joe, it's Joe.

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:02.540
And he would try and
get me to call him Joe.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:04.230
He does that with all his residents.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:06.120
The nicest program director ever.

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:09.670
Smart, probably the best,
I'm comfortable saying that,

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:12.270
the best bedside manner
I've seen in a physician.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:16.420
We talked afterwards on the
phone, both were really excited.

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.680
And it was time to start
my medical journey.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:23.223
I graduated, boom, I'm a
doctor, I got my DO degree.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:27.270
And I had basically a month to relax

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:29.100
before residency started.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:31.270
And I want to urge all of you.

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:34.770
If you have some time off
before your residency starts,

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:36.350
please enjoy it.

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:40.250
Go party, go explore, vacation,

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:44.080
whatever you need to do to
enjoy yourself, please do it.

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:44.913
Now I'm not saying that

00:10:44.913 --> 00:10:47.000
because residency's a
horrible time in your life,

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:49.010
but I will tell you it
is a stressful time.

00:10:49.010 --> 00:10:51.000
There's gonna be an insane time commitment

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:53.240
that you're gonna have to
dedicate to the program,

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:56.250
and it's needed, you need
to commit that much time

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:57.660
in order to have a solid foundation

00:10:57.660 --> 00:10:58.620
for your medical learning.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:01.190
I went on some vacations during that time.

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:03.330
So when I came into
residency, I was fresh.

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:04.170
That's what you wanna do.

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:07.070
You wanna come in fresh,
eager, ready to learn.

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:08.450
The last thing you wanna do

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:10.450
is overwhelm yourself
with research or something

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:13.480
and then come in completely
burned out into a place

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:16.260
where the burnout rate is
really high on its own.

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:18.880
Now, you're going into
residency and you're wondering,

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:21.550
what should I do so that I learn the most,

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:23.103
or that I'm prepared the most?

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:25.240
Nothing.

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:26.840
Yes, you can read some books,

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:29.600
you can look over some manuals,

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:33.090
and have some handouts in your
pockets in your white coat,

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:34.420
but nothing's gonna prepare you

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:36.140
for what you're gonna
experience in residency.

00:11:36.140 --> 00:11:37.780
I don't mean to sound dire,

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:40.300
it's not that crazy and dangerous.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:42.890
But residency's tricky.

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:45.070
You're gonna be interacting
with a lot of different people.

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:49.890
Nurses, doctors, MAs, janitorial staff,

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:54.880
phlebotomists, patients,
patients' family members,

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:56.840
insurance companies, I mean,

00:11:56.840 --> 00:11:58.250
the amount of people you communicate with

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:00.330
on a daily basis is gonna be amazing.

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:02.230
You're not gonna know
how to handle everything.

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:05.490
You have to not be
afraid to ask questions.

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:07.930
That's the number one thing in residency.

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:10.110
If you're confident
about something, great.

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:12.730
But if you're the slightest bit unsure,

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:16.960
either A, look it up, or
B, ask a senior for advice.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:19.090
Because the last thing you wanna do

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:22.000
is hurt a patient, hurt
a patient's family member

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:25.370
because you're too proud
to confirm your knowledge.

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:28.970
I despise that about some people

00:12:28.970 --> 00:12:31.860
that are what we call
cowboys in the field.

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:33.350
They think that they

00:12:33.350 --> 00:12:35.220
read something somewhere on the Internet,

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:36.840
now they know everything
about the subject,

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:38.750
and they're gonna write all these orders,

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:41.410
put in all these medications,
without double-checking.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:42.890
I double-checked everything,

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:44.930
even things I was
somewhat comfortable with.

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:46.690
I asked the senior to make sure

00:12:46.690 --> 00:12:48.350
that they saw me do the procedure,

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:49.760
saw me do the order right,

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.640
so I was treating the patient
to the best of my ability.

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:54.220
Obviously, as you get more comfortable

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:55.660
throughout your time in residency,

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:57.050
you'll be doing that less and less,

00:12:57.050 --> 00:12:58.790
and you're gonna be
teaching more and more.

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:00.910
When residency first started,

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:03.500
my first two months were
on the surgical floor.

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:05.800
I was basically an
acting surgery resident.

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:08.310
And what's interesting about
Overlook Medical Center

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:13.310
is that not only are Overlook
residents there doing surgery,

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:17.720
but also Columbia residents
are doing surgery there.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:20.620
And that's why, fun fact, Dr. Oz

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:23.470
actually did some of his training
at Overlook Medical Center

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:25.550
as part of his residency.

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:29.060
And basically, what
happens is as an intern,

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:34.020
you get partnered up with
a senior Columbia resident,

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:36.730
and they basically teach you the ropes,

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:40.883
allow you to carry the pager,
scrub in into some operations.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:44.460
Basically, jump in in the first week,

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:45.900
and you're taking care of patients.

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:47.610
Someone's having high blood pressure,

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:49.430
someone's becoming tachycardic,

00:13:49.430 --> 00:13:51.400
meaning their heart's beating fast,

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:52.233
bradycardic, the other way,

00:13:52.233 --> 00:13:53.870
their heart is beating really slow.

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:55.880
You're the one that's getting paged first,

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:57.900
you're the one responsible.

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:01.420
And while I was happy to
have that responsibility,

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:04.160
I was also nervous, and
it's okay to be nervous.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:06.860
Nervous energy can be good
energy if it's not chronic,

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:09.390
meaning it doesn't happen
over a long period of time.

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:11.540
You need to embrace that energy,

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:14.000
and say, this energy, this anxiety,

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.110
is preparing me to take on this challenge

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:19.260
of taking on these pages, these
patients, and all of that.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:21.320
Initially, when I got some of those pages,

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:23.750
I may have been a little too nervous,

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:27.120
and maybe asked my
seniors for too much help,

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:28.880
just because I wasn't confident,

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:31.470
especially because during my fourth year,

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:33.610
I did a lot of family medicine rotations

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:35.550
where you don't do a lot of surgery,

00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:38.980
and I was doing that because
I wanted to figure out

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:42.920
where I wanted to end up
in terms of my residency.

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:43.753
But because that,

00:14:43.753 --> 00:14:45.260
I didn't have a lot of
surgical experience.

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:46.790
So I relied very heavily

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:50.300
on the Columbia surgical
residents to help me out.

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:52.290
But then as the weeks went by,

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:55.280
I became more and more comfortable
in managing the patients.

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:57.930
I became more and more
comfortable answering pages,

00:14:57.930 --> 00:15:00.800
and it's really important,
because when you're on call,

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:03.470
it's the middle of the
night, you get a page,

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:04.710
you have to answer it,

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:08.210
your senior is likely scrubbed
in in the middle of the night

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:11.530
in an intense surgery,
and so is the attending.

00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:14.030
So, you don't really have
anyone to go for answers,

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:17.090
unless it's obviously a dire emergency.

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:20.240
So you get very good at using
some of the hospital resources

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:25.240
like UpToDate, which is basically
a system that we look for

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:29.190
for evidence-based medicine information.

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:31.860
For example, if I saw a patient

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:35.990
that was post-surgically having a fever,

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:38.630
you can go on UpToDate,
search post-surgical fever,

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:39.970
and get some information about it

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:42.550
that could help guide your
workup, your treatment,

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:43.600
and so on, so forth.

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:46.530
This is where it's probably
really important to mention

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:51.320
that during med school, I
started an instagram account.

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:54.340
And initially, it was
dr.mikeee or something.

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:59.340
It was like dr_mike with three
Es or something like that,

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:02.320
because I wanted to show what it was like

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:06.230
to be just an ordinary medical
student, nothing special.

00:16:06.230 --> 00:16:08.470
But be able to enjoy your life.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:13.470
Be able to have a balance
between exercise, education,

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:18.690
social life, going out,
enjoying nice events,

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:20.040
but still doing well in school,

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:22.380
because I feel there exists a stigma,

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:24.060
and I really think it's a stigma,

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:26.640
that if you go into medical school,

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:28.510
you have to give up your life.

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:31.400
Myth busted, that is complete BS.

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:34.070
Everybody needs to study
very hard in medical school,

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:36.100
but it doesn't mean that you
have to give up your life.

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:38.460
You can study hard, you can study smart,

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:41.030
and you can do very well while
still enjoying your life.

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:42.080
So what I was doing,

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:46.010
I was sort of doing a,
what was popular back then,

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:49.480
a lot of those, not MySpace,
it came after MySpace,

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:50.830
but it was one of those...

00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:57.120
It was like, not a blog, it was...

00:16:57.120 --> 00:16:57.953
Xanga.

00:16:57.953 --> 00:16:59.320
Basically, what I was doing

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:02.420
was showing my life through the Internet.

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:04.010
I was living my life through the Internet,

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:05.100
and I was blogging.

00:17:05.100 --> 00:17:07.420
This was a new thing
that people were doing

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:10.070
in the mid to early 2000s.

00:17:10.070 --> 00:17:12.970
Xanga, LiveJournal, these
were popular blogging sites.

00:17:12.970 --> 00:17:15.230
But I was like, I wanna
do that on Instagram.

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:17.050
I wanna do a photo blog of my life.

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:19.760
So I took selfies of me
in the operating room,

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:23.150
I took selfies with my dog,
I took selfies with my dad,

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:25.730
at fancy events throughout New York City,

00:17:25.730 --> 00:17:28.950
and people really
enjoyed what I was doing.

00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:32.090
So I built up a pretty
significant following

00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:33.150
for the average person,

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:37.200
somewhere 20, 30,000 followers
going into residency.

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:40.150
It was a running joke
during my intern year

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:42.730
that people that knew me well,
they would make jokes like,

00:17:42.730 --> 00:17:45.030
oh, Dr. Mike has a lot
of followers on Instagram

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:47.550
with his 20,000, he was a celebrity.

00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:49.750
And look, we had fun with it.

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:53.040
The nurses had fun with it, my
co-residents had fun with it.

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:55.480
Occasionally, they would
catch one of their friends

00:17:55.480 --> 00:17:57.760
following me, and we
would tease them about it,

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:00.000
but whatever, it was all in good fun.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:02.030
I thought it was something
useful that I was doing,

00:18:02.030 --> 00:18:06.120
showing medical students what
it was like to be a doctor.

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:09.090
Maybe people that just enjoyed medicine.

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:10.250
And I thought that in the future,

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:11.860
maybe this could be of some value.

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:13.990
I didn't know exactly
how we could be of value,

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:15.220
but I thought that it was nice

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:17.880
that I could communicate with
thousands of people at home

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:20.840
just how I do with everybody
in my everyday life.

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:25.400
So despite calls from
some people saying that

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:27.470
taking selfies was unprofessional,

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:29.720
or being on social media
was unprofessional,

00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:32.700
I saw a greater use for it in the future.

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:33.770
So I left it.

00:18:33.770 --> 00:18:35.450
It didn't take up much time.

00:18:35.450 --> 00:18:37.360
I would take a selfie here and there.

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:40.053
Most people never saw me take a selfie.

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:43.150
My dad didn't even know
I had social media,

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:45.670
because prior to this,
I never even had Twitter

00:18:45.670 --> 00:18:47.180
or Instagram or any of this stuff.

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:50.330
I don't think I ever overstepped the line,

00:18:50.330 --> 00:18:54.650
and gave an unrealistic
view of what I was doing.

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:57.120
I think that when you saw me
smiling in those pictures,

00:18:57.120 --> 00:18:58.910
I wasn't just smiling for the pictures.

00:18:58.910 --> 00:19:01.830
I was smiling with my residents
a few moments before that.

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:04.663
I worked really hard,
intern year is crazy.

00:19:04.663 --> 00:19:08.260
I've worked 36-hour shifts.

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:11.150
When you're in the ICU, you're
working six days a week,

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:13.500
on call every other day.

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:14.870
Meaning that when you're on call,

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:17.340
not only do you commit at 4:30, 5:00 A.M.,

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:19.130
but you go home at 9:00 P.M.

00:19:19.130 --> 00:19:20.510
Just think about how little time

00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:23.060
that leaves for you to sleep
or take care of yourself.

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:25.520
It's a really grueling schedule.

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:27.960
During that month, you learn a lot.

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:29.900
You do that rotation I believe two times

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:32.610
in the first of the year,
at least from my program.

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:37.610
Boom, I'm in my second year,
and you get four weeks off.

00:19:37.780 --> 00:19:39.300
I think this is a requirement.

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:43.330
You get four weeks off a
year during your residency.

00:19:43.330 --> 00:19:45.730
And during my first year,

00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:47.060
I didn't really have vacations

00:19:47.060 --> 00:19:49.410
towards the tail-end
of that residency year,

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:53.130
so I did my first week of vacation

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:56.830
in August, September of my second year.

00:19:56.830 --> 00:19:59.600
And guess what, I'm on
vacation, I'm in Florida,

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:01.630
and all of a sudden, one
of my buddies texts me,

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:04.650
and he said, hey man, you're on BuzzFeed.

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:06.610
And I'm like, oh man, that's really cool,

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:09.970
because while I've done
some little news articles

00:20:09.970 --> 00:20:12.670
here and there, I've done
some scientific publications,

00:20:12.670 --> 00:20:15.460
I've never had something as
big as BuzzFeed mention me.

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:16.880
And I was like, oh, that's cool.

00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:18.130
I read the story,

00:20:18.130 --> 00:20:20.260
it wasn't anything about
my medical accomplishments,

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:22.230
it was hot doctor and husky.

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:23.880
And I was like, oh, that's nice.

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:26.500
Awesome compliment, pretty exciting.

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:28.080
I didn't think anything
would come from it.

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:30.290
Then as the day went on,

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:32.930
I started getting more and
more texts from people.

00:20:32.930 --> 00:20:36.070
Not only about BuzzFeed
now, now Elite Daily,

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:40.420
then Huffington Post,
then AOL.com, then Time,

00:20:40.420 --> 00:20:42.560
then Cosmo, then Glamour,

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:46.130
and all of a sudden, my
inbox starts imploding.

00:20:46.130 --> 00:20:49.170
I mean, everybody's emailing me that week.

00:20:49.170 --> 00:20:51.920
Ellen DeGeneres people,
Steve Harvey people.

00:20:51.920 --> 00:20:55.190
Everybody is saying, if you do our show,

00:20:55.190 --> 00:20:58.600
you can do our show, but
you can't do their show.

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:00.480
If you come on Fox, you can't do CBS.

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:02.310
By the way, I don't remember
exactly who said what,

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:05.100
but this was all being said.

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:06.560
And I'm a resident.

00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:08.250
I know nothing about media!

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:11.080
I have no representation,
I have no guides.

00:21:11.080 --> 00:21:13.550
I don't even know anyone
in the industry, really.

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:17.420
I was faced with a choice,
because I'm a professional,

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:18.990
I'm going into a professional field,

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:23.990
and I had to decide, do
you embrace this hot topic,

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:26.110
sorry, this hot doctor topic,

00:21:26.110 --> 00:21:27.640
as something that you wanna go

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:31.330
in front of millions of people
on television and talk about

00:21:31.330 --> 00:21:33.310
as if it's something you truly care about.

00:21:33.310 --> 00:21:36.360
I didn't really care that people
were calling me hot doctor.

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:39.240
But what I did care was that suddenly,

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:42.210
millions of people were
interested to listen to me.

00:21:42.210 --> 00:21:44.290
And I viewed that as an opportunity.

00:21:44.290 --> 00:21:45.740
Honestly, it was insane.

00:21:45.740 --> 00:21:49.820
I gained 750,000 followers
in those two weeks.

00:21:49.820 --> 00:21:51.670
My phone was just, (tongue trilling).

00:21:52.790 --> 00:21:54.470
I had to turn off notifications

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:55.540
for the first time in my life.

00:21:55.540 --> 00:21:56.840
I've never had to do that for anything,

00:21:56.840 --> 00:21:59.480
because so many things were coming in.

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:03.270
I was answering news agencies at the time.

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:04.250
And it's funny.

00:22:04.250 --> 00:22:05.750
Because I was on vacation,

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:08.270
and wasn't able to do
a lot of the news stuff

00:22:08.270 --> 00:22:09.920
till the following week,

00:22:09.920 --> 00:22:11.840
I reached out to them the following week,

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:13.200
said sorry, it's been so crazy,

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:14.740
I would like to come on this week.

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:16.640
They were like, oh no,
you're old news, man.

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:19.410
You were on last week, we
talked about you last week.

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:20.780
This week, we have no use for you.

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:24.960
And I realized how cut-throat
the industry was very quickly.

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:27.810
Now, I'm getting these opportunities,

00:22:27.810 --> 00:22:29.990
my Instagram is exploding.

00:22:29.990 --> 00:22:32.170
What I felt like I had
to do with social media

00:22:32.170 --> 00:22:34.280
was find a positive outlet for it.

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:36.750
How can I get people that are watching

00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:39.760
nonsense Kim Kardashian videos,

00:22:39.760 --> 00:22:42.630
and listening to "Percocet,
Molly, Percocet,"

00:22:42.630 --> 00:22:45.340
how can I get those
people to watch my content

00:22:45.340 --> 00:22:49.210
where we can have some fun,
I'll dance like I'm a loser,

00:22:49.210 --> 00:22:51.200
whatever, have some fun, get some laughs,

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:53.270
but also learn something in the process?

00:22:53.270 --> 00:22:55.230
I can talk about
"Percocet, Molly, Percocet"

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:56.350
and dance to it,

00:22:56.350 --> 00:22:57.877
but I can explain to you what Percocet

00:22:57.877 --> 00:22:59.260
and Molly Percocet are.

00:22:59.260 --> 00:23:03.240
I try to find a balance between
social media and learning.

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:05.280
How can we make those two things coexist?

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:10.280
From that moment, that was in
2015, it's January 2018 now,

00:23:10.350 --> 00:23:12.230
a lot has transpired.

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:13.450
I started a YouTube channel,

00:23:13.450 --> 00:23:15.620
that's how you're watching me now.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:17.840
I've done a lot of television.

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:20.110
My social media has sort of

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:21.720
transformed outside of just Instagram.

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:23.270
It's on Facebook, Twitter,

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:25.490
even I do some little
Snapchat here and there.

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:28.760
I do see a lot of people asking questions

00:23:28.760 --> 00:23:33.160
and making statements
about how I am a doctor,

00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:35.330
but yet, I am also a marketer,

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:38.510
because I do do advertisements
on my social media,

00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:41.220
and some of them say, oh,
this isn't medically ethical

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:43.330
for you to be a good
marketer and a doctor.

00:23:43.330 --> 00:23:46.300
Those two things cannot coexist.

00:23:46.300 --> 00:23:48.300
I understand where that
sentiment comes from.

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:50.670
I see the TV doctors
that are selling nonsense

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:52.320
and compromising their medical ethics.

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:55.800
I totally understand that, and
I agree with you, to a point.

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:59.660
When I do an advertisement,
for let's say a watch company,

00:23:59.660 --> 00:24:01.840
I don't think that
compromises my medical ethics,

00:24:01.840 --> 00:24:04.350
because when I
introspectively look at that,

00:24:04.350 --> 00:24:05.920
me recommending to wear a watch,

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:07.410
or not even recommending to wear a watch,

00:24:07.410 --> 00:24:10.480
me just saying I like this
watch, or I wear this watch,

00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:14.250
has no impact on our
doctor-patient relationship.

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:15.790
Another place where I think

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:18.540
that marketing can even
be good for medicine

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:21.320
is that when I do an ad for let's say,

00:24:21.320 --> 00:24:24.403
Charmin reached out to me once
and I did an ad for Charmin,

00:24:25.310 --> 00:24:27.010
they wanted me to take a picture

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:28.870
in front of their Charmin on the go van

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:31.330
where people go take poops, literally,

00:24:31.330 --> 00:24:32.730
on the street, it's pretty funny.

00:24:32.730 --> 00:24:34.640
But it's part of their advertising.

00:24:34.640 --> 00:24:36.050
They wanted me to take a picture of that

00:24:36.050 --> 00:24:38.050
and show that to my Instagram community.

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:39.970
I wanted to take the picture,

00:24:39.970 --> 00:24:41.900
but I also wanted to use this opportunity

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:43.700
to educate you at home,

00:24:43.700 --> 00:24:46.440
and talk about the
different signs and symptoms

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:47.740
that you should look out for

00:24:47.740 --> 00:24:49.440
that might mean something serious,

00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:51.690
or warrant further evaluation.

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:52.640
So I told them, yes,

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:54.240
I'll take a picture in front of the truck,

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:56.190
but I'm gonna tell people
to look at their poop,

00:24:56.190 --> 00:24:58.380
and look at the different
changes in their poops

00:24:58.380 --> 00:25:00.680
that should require
them to go see a doctor.

00:25:00.680 --> 00:25:01.930
So I did two things in that.

00:25:01.930 --> 00:25:04.370
I advertised what they
needed me to advertise.

00:25:04.370 --> 00:25:07.380
I made money that I can use
for this YouTube channel,

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:09.940
to educate you and
continue doing what I do,

00:25:09.940 --> 00:25:11.880
to make money for myself,
to feed my family,

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:14.820
to feed myself, and pay
off my student loans,

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:17.260
and I was able to get
some education across.

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:19.530
Again, I only do advertising for companies

00:25:19.530 --> 00:25:21.920
that I believe in, that
I would actually use.

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:23.510
I wouldn't, you know,

00:25:23.510 --> 00:25:25.760
advertise some crazy creepy products

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:27.640
that I've been pitched in the past.

00:25:27.640 --> 00:25:29.410
I mean, I've been pitched

00:25:29.410 --> 00:25:32.750
just the nuttiest things in the world.

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:35.070
Obviously, I've gotten seven-figure offers

00:25:35.070 --> 00:25:36.447
from vitamin supplement companies

00:25:36.447 --> 00:25:40.160
that want me to say this
is the next best thing,

00:25:40.160 --> 00:25:42.693
this is what's gonna cure what ails you.

00:25:43.632 --> 00:25:46.750
This is the solution,
I will never do that.

00:25:46.750 --> 00:25:49.890
Why, because as a doctor,
I want proof that it works,

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:51.890
and if it works, I'll
recommend that as a doctor.

00:25:51.890 --> 00:25:53.720
I don't need to be paid to do it.

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:56.260
That's my job, I'm
already paid to do that.

00:25:56.260 --> 00:25:57.940
I really work hard to make sure

00:25:57.940 --> 00:25:59.360
that the things that I do advertise

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:01.750
do not cross those medical lines.

00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:03.810
Lines sometimes do get blurry,

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:06.360
but again, I will always
make my best effort

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:07.630
to make sure I never cross that,

00:26:07.630 --> 00:26:09.140
because I am a doctor first,

00:26:09.140 --> 00:26:12.230
well, I'm a human first,
but I'm a doctor second,

00:26:12.230 --> 00:26:14.010
and that's what's most important to me.

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:17.170
Your health, our trust that
we form between each other

00:26:17.170 --> 00:26:18.230
is really important to me,

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:20.730
and I think that when you're
a social media personality,

00:26:20.730 --> 00:26:22.320
that trust is even more important.

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:24.440
I do have some more stuff to talk about.

00:26:24.440 --> 00:26:28.120
Doing television, I do a lot of TV now.

00:26:28.120 --> 00:26:32.130
Good Morning America, The
Doctors show, Rachael Ray.

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:34.680
And that's a whole
different animal on its own.

00:26:34.680 --> 00:26:36.610
And I'm also learning that process.

00:26:36.610 --> 00:26:39.100
So if you wanna know
more about that process,

00:26:39.100 --> 00:26:40.500
and how I do it, and what I do,

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:42.250
and what my journey's been like,

00:26:42.250 --> 00:26:45.350
comment below, give this video a like,

00:26:45.350 --> 00:26:49.600
and continue supporting good creators.

00:26:49.600 --> 00:26:50.860
'Cause that's what it's about.

00:26:50.860 --> 00:26:52.520
It's about having a community

00:26:52.520 --> 00:26:54.690
where we're all sharing our experiences.

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:58.040
Those who are good storytellers
continue being heard,

00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:00.340
and I really appreciate what you guys do

00:27:00.340 --> 00:27:03.000
by watching my channel,
sharing my channel,

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:04.310
liking and commenting.

00:27:04.310 --> 00:27:06.700
I try to make a really serious effort

00:27:06.700 --> 00:27:09.660
to not only like and heart
everybody's comments,

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:12.550
but also jump in and
reply as much as I can.

00:27:12.550 --> 00:27:14.600
I know that some of you have seen that,

00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:17.520
and they may not be paragraph replies,

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:19.040
but again remember, I am a doctor,

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:20.950
I am bouncing around from place to place,

00:27:20.950 --> 00:27:22.880
so I do try my best.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:24.930
As always, guys, thanks for watching.

00:27:24.930 --> 00:27:26.790
Stay happy and healthy.

00:27:26.790 --> 00:27:28.071
I didn't move my chair, did I?

00:27:28.071 --> 00:27:29.230
(chair creaking)

00:27:29.230 --> 00:27:30.850
This sound isn't annoying?

00:27:30.850 --> 00:27:31.683
Water, please.

00:27:33.655 --> 00:27:34.884
Pew whoo!

00:27:34.884 --> 00:27:37.551
(hip hop music)

